## Code Example
```c
int ctz64(uint64_t n) {
  // Implementation uses de Bruijn indexing
  uint64_t t = n & -n;
  // table[index] yields trailing-zero count
  return table[(t * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a01ULL) >> 58];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Rotations are fundamental in cryptography (e.g., SHA-2), hashing, and bitboard manipulation. Implementing them correctly on composite 128-bit types tests understanding of cross-word boundaries and careful handling of edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Decompose 128-bit value into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Handle k near 64 where rotation crosses half boundaries
- No loops required—just careful bitwise arithmetic
- Return early for k==0 to avoid undefined behavior with 64-bit shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotl128(uint128 x, unsigned k) {
    k &= 127;
    if (k == 0) return x;

    if (k < 64) {
        uint64_t hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
        uint64_t lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    } else {
        unsigned k2 = k - 64;
        uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k2) | (x.hi >> (64 - k2));
        uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k2) | (x.lo >> (64 - k2));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) bit counting and handling of 128-bit data without branching.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR-based popcount
- 128-bit data split into 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling tricks

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned pop64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (unsigned)(v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

