## Why This Is Asked
Transposition in bit-sliced form is a classic challenge that tests mastery of layout tricks and how data moves across a 128-bit container without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice layout and Morton-order ideas
- Multi-stage bit redistribution with masks
- Branchless, vector-style bit manipulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline for guidance
uint128 transpose8x16(uint128 x) {
  // stage 1: swap adjacent bits
  x = (x & 0x5555...5555) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555...5555);
  // stage 2: swap pairs
  x = (x & 0x3333...3333) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333...3333);
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x & 0x0F0F...0F0F) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F...0F0F);
  // stage 4: move bytes
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of 128-bit bitboards, branchless kth-one, and cross-half boundary without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; popcount and select in constant time
- Branchless kth-one within a 64-bit word
- Correct handling when n is out of range across halves

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
int findNthOne128(uint128 a, unsigned n){
  unsigned loCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.lo);
  unsigned hiCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.hi);
  unsigned total = loCnt + hiCnt;
  if (n >= total) return -1;
  if (n < loCnt) return kth1_64(a.lo, n);
  return 64 + kth1_64(a.hi, n - loCnt);
}
```

